WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the program we begin in  afghanistan where the first civilian  flight has just taken off from kabul's  airport since the capital fell to the  taliban last month these are exclusive  pictures of the plane leaving  it's a domestic service bound for the  northern city of mazari sharif  its departure follows repairs to the  runway and radar with the help of  technical teams from qatar  and turkey there it goes taking off now  the first flight let's get more on that  story from charles stratford who joins  us now from kabul and of course  charles we were hearing uh for several  days that technical teams were trying to  get the airport open again with the  airport opens it means  communication really not just  domestically but potentially with the  rest of the world as well  that's right sohail but obviously it's  important to recognize there's a long  way to go yet um we understand that the  the airport was in a terrible state of  disrepair and as you say  we've seen dozens of members of  technical teams from qatar we understand  turkey as well that have been busy over  the last few days getting some of the  fundamentals  in terms of equipment and a radar system  repairing the runway  ready for this yes not insignificant  moment that first domestic flight to the  northern town of  sharif we also know that um  qatar sent a or there was a an a flight  in from qatar today the first in from  qatar there was one in from the uae  yesterday but according to the country  ambassador he's very hopeful that  country ambassador was speaking on the  tarmac um just before that flight took  off very hopeful that uh today signifies  yeah a bit of a a bit of a watershed  and as you say communication with the  outside world is renewed via that  airport of kiosk there's vast amounts of  humanitarian aid that needs to come into  this country the un are estimating that  around half the population of  afghanistan needs urgent humanitarian  aid that's around 20 million people so  it's vital that this good work continues  and that we see obviously those large  planes those c-17s and  c-130s that we've seen that were very  much involved in that evacuation effort  bringing in that vital aid  in the coming days and of course charles  in another story that we're keeping a  very close eye on is a situation north  of the capital in the panshire valley  and conflicting reports about who's  actually in control and who's made gains  that's right things got very messy to  put a a  not too blunt a point on it last night  uh when we heard  certainly from unnamed taliban  commanders reportedly in that area that  the taliban are taking full control of  the panchayat valley that was swiftly  refuted a couple of hours later by  um a video that was leaked to local  media we understand by the former vice  president of ruler salah who we  understand or claims to be inside the  panshir valley he was saying that these  claims by the taliban were in his words  completely baseless and that resistance  would continue now  uh we know that the valley is is very  easy or is very well defended sorry by  this opposition group to the taliban we  also according to the taliban say they  they say that they have fully surrounded  the area the big concern is for what we  understand are around 150 to 200 000  civilians that are inside the valley  um mainly from a tajik the tagic ethnic  minority  we got very close to the entrance  yesterday  we spoke to civilians in that area and  the taliban and this is our report  smoke rises over the town of gulbahar  the entrance to the panchir valley in  northeast afghanistan  neither the soviets during the 1980s war  nor the taliban have ever managed to  control this mountainous region around  100 kilometers north of kabul  a shell lands nearby  taliban fighters ready their weapons  men in this village say they have driven  their wives and children to safety they  are angry and scared  the americans betrayed us president  ghani betrayed us too  you left us under the cannons and tanks  and we can't stop either side fighting  how much can they kill their brothers  how much can they kill their fellow  afghans  they have to stop fighting  the taliban drive american military  vehicles and pick up trucks taking  fighters to and from the front line  thousands of men like these across  afghanistan have known little else but  conflict and war all their lives  the fighting started three days ago now  we've taken a lot of areas up there by  the antenna we have no issue with the  civilians we are here to defend their  rights people are staying in their homes  the national resistance front of  afghanistan or nrf is believed to be  several thousand strong and made up of  mainly ethnic tajik fighters and former  afghan army soldiers the group is led by  this man ahmed masood  he's british military academy trained  and the son of ahmed sharmasood who  fought against the soviets and the  taliban and was assassinated by al-qaeda  two days before the 9 11 attacks in  2001.  ahmed massoud is demanding the taliban  form an inclusive government which  guarantees the rights of all  afghanistan's ethnic minorities  including ethnic tajics who make up  around a quarter of the country's  population  the fighting started after two weeks of  negotiations with the taliban collapsed  we have a lot of equipment with  ourselves we will defend from our willy  from our territory until we have one  blood of one drop of blood in our body  so we will resist until we martial in  here taliban fighters edge up a slope  along tracks they say are heavily mined  as we were getting ready to go live  another shell landed close by  the taliban has blocked phone networks  in the valley there are growing fears  about the estimated 150 000 people  living inside and many in taliban  controlled areas around gulbahar  the big concern is that massoud's forces  are coming round on the mountain ridges  trying to surround this area in order to  isolate gulbahar  we left in gulbahar but now we are  leaving  today i came from kabul to collect our  belongings  i already took my family out the  fighting is ongoing there are shells  hitting people's homes both sides are  challenged  afghan families flee for their lives in  one of the last pockets of resistance to  taliban rule  analysts are telling us that they  believe that the nrf are seriously well  armed  they say that uh  that group um or certainly the panchiris  as many people call them have been  stockpiling weapons in the panchi valley  for decades um as we heard there  the panchiris were never taking control  over the valley was never controlled by  the soviets nor the taliban speaking to  locals yesterday in that area it was  interesting they were saying that they'd  seen a lot of weapons go into the valley  in and around the days of when the  taliban came into kabul so they were  very much prepared  but also to be remembered is the fact  that they are massively outnumbered by  the taliban so yeah  huge ramifications for the civilian  population  in and around that area and also very  interesting as well people that we've  been speaking to we've been obviously  speculating as to when this new taliban  government will be formed and  announcements on that well many experts  saying they don't expect potentially any  new government to be announced until the  panshir fighting is over  because so they say they want the the  taliban want to be seen as fully  inclusive and of course there is a lot  of ethnic tashicks in that area so yeah  that conflict 100 or so kilometers north  of kabul has huge ramifications not only  with respect to a humanitarian level and  the suffering of thousands of people but  you know the political future of this  country and of course we'll continue to  monitor what happens there north of the  capital with you charles stratford in  kabul thank you

